Save93 equipment runs with the test control "skip blocked" (VMCatchUp::SetSkipBlocked), 2026-11-10 18:42 build / AE 1.6.1170 / limiter 30 FPS, DynamicUpdateBudget=false / Papyrus bEnableLogging=0, bEnableProfiling=1
Test DLL APBGameplayTrace.dll sha256 fde28133c5d42254cc0320a2be82f4b778c29b6130857a4ae26327da207f2267 (release DLL of the same source bbd3f0ee..., which never calls the control). APB_CATCHUP_MODE=accelerate, target 60, engage below 50, reviewed-read rule with property reads.
With the control on, an extra cycle does not end at a call that must wait: it moves that call behind the others and runs the calls that may run; afterwards the moved calls are at the front again in their original order. The rule "the front of the queue is never skipped" is given up.
  sk1-30, sk2-30   catchup-skipfront: catch-up on in every trial; "on" = skip blocked, "off" = release behaviour.
  skab-30-1, skab-30-2   catchup-ab with -SkipBlocked: "on" = catch-up on with skip blocked, "off" = catch-up off.
First in-game runs of the code that takes messages out of the native-call queue and puts them back. No crash; guard_violations and overrun_pauses 0 in all 32 trials; no "turned off" warning; handler counts and matched equipment requests as in the other runs; foreground_pct 100.0 in all 32.

RESULT, drain of the equipment handlers (ms after the last signal; same handler count):
  catch-up off vs on+skip (skab): 174 handlers off 1322-1358 (4 trials, mean 1332) / on 292, 294, 423 (mean 337, -75%); 202-203 handlers off 1956-2023 (4 trials, mean 1982) / on 720-924 (5 trials, mean 850, -57%).
  skip off vs on, catch-up on in both (sk1, sk2): 174 handlers off 1091-1293 (6 trials, mean 1180) / on 325, 391 (mean 358, -70%); 202-203 handlers off 1659 (1 trial; another off trial had 213 handlers, 1820) / on 658-991 (6 trials, mean 863).
RESULT, queue wait of every queued native call of every script (see below): catch-up off 33.8 and 39.9 ms / on+skip 24.9 and 22.4 ms; same-frame share 9% -> 36%. Release rule without skip 32.3 and 29.5 ms / with skip 22.4 and 24.9 ms.
COST: extra time per frame 0.11-0.14 ms (0.05-0.07 without skip), largest single frame 0.97 ms; an extra cycle ran in 237-238 of 239 frames (107-144 without skip); 955-1530 calls moved behind per trial. Engine frame time p99 35.58-35.68 off / 35.70-35.81 on. Utility.Wait response p95 unchanged (59.4-62.0 ms).
NOTE: the drain is a backlog (work that piled up behind the last equipment change), so it can fall by more than the per-call wait does.

